Bima's device starts with an empty workspace. In this tutorial, both teammates
work together to copy the project identity and the two shared sites from Heru's
device to Bima's by scanning QR codes, ensuring both devices hold the same
records with matching UUIDs.

## 1. Copy the project identity

{% steps %}

1. **Heru:** Open the `Dashboard` and tap the QR code icon in
   `Project overview`.
2. **Bima:** Select `Create project` > `Scan project QR`, then point the camera
   at Heru's screen.
3. **Bima:** Confirm that `Project UUID` matches Heru's device, then select
   `Create project`.

{% /steps %}

{% aside type="tip" title="No camera on Bima's device?" %}
If Bima is using a laptop or desktop without QR scanning, Heru can export the
project info as a JSON file from `Project overview` and share it. Bima then
selects `Create project` > `Import project-info JSON` instead.
{% /aside %}

{% devicepair
   tablet="app-screenshots/tutorials/teamwork/ipad-project-qr.webp"
   tabletAlt="NAHPU Project QR code dialog on an iPad with the caption: scan this code when creating a new project to transfer project information"
   tabletLabel="Heru · iPad"
   phone="app-screenshots/tutorials/teamwork/iphone-create-project.webp"
   phoneAlt="NAHPU Create project screen on an iPhone with Create new project, Import project-info JSON, and Scan project QR"
   phoneLabel="Bima · iPhone" /%}

The review screen summarizes what the QR code carries: the project UUID, name,
description, time zone, and dates. It also notes what is left out (i.e.,
records, personnel, taxa, and device settings), which you will share next.

{% devicepair
   phone="app-screenshots/tutorials/teamwork/iphone-review-project.webp"
   phoneAlt="NAHPU Review imported project on an iPhone with project UUID 3f6c2a8e-9b41-4d7e-a5c2-71e0d4b8c913, name Tujuh teamwork 2014, time zone Asia/Jakarta, and dates 12 to 18 September 2014"
   phoneLabel="Bima · iPhone" /%}

## 2. Copy the shared sites

{% steps %}

1. **Heru:** Open `Sites`, navigate to `TJHH-L1`, and select `Show QR` from the
   three-dot menu (or `Export site` if sharing as a JSON file).
2. **Bima:** Open `Sites` and select `Scan QR` (or `Import site`) from the
   three-dot menu.
3. **Bima:** In `Import site`, keep `Create new site` selected and choose
   `Continue`.
4. **Both:** Repeat steps 1–3 for `TJHH-P1`.

{% /steps %}

{% devicepair
   phone="app-screenshots/tutorials/teamwork/iphone-site-menu.webp"
   phoneAlt="NAHPU Sites menu on an iPhone with Show QR and Export site unavailable, and Scan QR and Import site available"
   phoneLabel="Bima · iPhone" /%}

{% aside type="caution" title="Select Create new site for the second site" %}
When a site is already open on the receiving device, `Import site` defaults to
`Update an existing site`, which would overwrite `TJHH-L1` with `TJHH-P1`.
Before continuing on the second site, switch the option to `Create new site`.
{% /aside %}

{% devicepair
   phone="app-screenshots/tutorials/teamwork/iphone-site-import.webp"
   phoneAlt="NAHPU Import site dialog on an iPhone for TJHH-P1 with one coordinate and one person, reusing the saved Pondok Merah locality, and Create new site selected instead of updating TJHH-L1"
   phoneLabel="Bima · iPhone" /%}

Each site transfer carries its geography, habitat, coordinates, and site
leader. The second site automatically reuses the Pondok Merah locality saved by
the first, leaving Bima's device with one locality and two sites, identical to
Heru's. Heru's personnel record arrives automatically as the site leader.

